From b1b21023e8862ae0306127c0791742baa2901ce7 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: Paul Junod <donujp@gmail.com>
Date: Sat, 10 Oct 2026 16:50:20 -0400
Subject: [PATCH 08/10] clutter/color: Add a fallible definition of the linear
 BT.2020 matrices

ClutterColorTransform built the matrices for a parametric color state
from clutter_colorimetry_to_XYZ(), clutter_colorimetry_from_XYZ() and a
private Bradford adaptation, none of which can report failure:

 - collinear primaries make the primaries matrix singular, and
   clutter_colorimetry_to_XYZ() then returns the identity matrix, so the
   content is silently rendered as if it were XYZ;
 - a white point with y = 0 is patched to y = FLT_EPSILON by
   clutter_xyY_to_XYZ(), producing enormous but "valid" numbers;
 - a non-finite adaptation (a white point with a zero cone response)
   propagates Inf/NaN into the pipeline unnoticed.

There was also no way for a producer of color states to ask whether a
colorimetry is usable without re-implementing this math, so validation
and rendering could not share one definition of supported colorimetry.

Add clutter_colorimetry_get_linear_bt2020_matrices() in a new,
uninstalled clutter-color-utils-private.h. It computes both directions
of the chain the transform uses (RGB -> XYZ -> Bradford adaptation to
D65 -> BT.2020, and the reverse) and accepts the colorimetry only if:

 - the primaries span a color space;
 - the white point has a positive, finite y, and is a positive
   combination of the three primaries, so every primary gets a positive
   scale (a white point on or outside their triangle does not);
 - both white points of each Bradford adaptation have positive cone
   responses, so every gain is positive and finite;
 - the two resulting single precision matrices are each other's inverse
   within 1e-4 in every element, evaluated in double precision.

The last criterion is needed because graphene_matrix_inverse() accepts
any matrix with |det| >= FLT_EPSILON, a threshold that does not scale
with the matrix: nearly collinear primaries pass it and get inverses
that are far from exact (a round trip off by 0.1). Well-conditioned
spaces stay below 2e-6, including the named color spaces, ACES AP0 and
AP1, ProPhoto, XYZ with an E or D65 white point, and large imaginary
gamuts; their matrices are bit-identical to those the transform used
before. Imaginary primaries (coordinates outside [0, 1]) remain valid
input.

The transform now takes its matrices from that function only. The
Bradford adaptation moved from clutter-color-transform.c as the
library-internal clutter_chromatic_adaptation_matrix(); the ICC path,
which adapts between the fixed D50 and D65 white points, keeps calling
it through a local wrapper.

clutter_colorimetry_get_linear_bt2020_matrices() is exported for
libmutter's validation of client and EDID colorimetry. The installed
clutter-color-utils.h is unchanged, and clutter_xyY_to_XYZ(),
clutter_colorimetry_to_XYZ() and clutter_colorimetry_from_XYZ() keep
their signatures and their historical warning-and-identity behaviour for
external callers; the latter two now share the primaries matrix
construction with the checked path.

If an unusable color state that bypassed validation still reaches the
transform, it is reported with a warning naming the state, once per
color state, and no matrix is substituted.

Related: https://gitlab.gnome.org/GNOME/mutter/-/issues/5001
